  "raw": "{\n  \"audits\": [\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q001\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_p110\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [\"cnm_p106\"],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"C: 4\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"The right middle box is 600 ÷ 30 = 20. Since that box is the product of 5 and the missing top number, the missing number is 20 ÷ 5 = 4. Recovering an unknown factor directly assesses cnm_p110, and the supporting calculation 600 ÷ 30 fits cnm_p106.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 1,\n          \"fact\": \"The top row contains 6, 5, and the missing number. The middle-left box contains 30 and touches the top boxes containing 6 and 5.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 1,\n          \"fact\": \"The bottom box contains 600 and touches both middle boxes; the middle-right box touches 5 and the missing top number.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q002\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_h181\", \"cnm_174\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"B: (√5 − 1)/2\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Each semicircle has diameter 1 and radius 1/2. Place the square at [0,2] × [0,2]. External tangency to equal-radius semicircles makes the target center equidistant from their centers. The symmetric pairs force this center to be (1,1). Its distance to the semicircle center (1/2,0) is d = √(1² + (1/2)²) = √5/2. External tangency gives R + 1/2 = d, so R = (√5 − 1)/2. The center-distance calculation and the sum-of-radii tangency relation match the two assessed scopes.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 2,\n          \"fact\": \"Each square side supports two inward semicircles whose diameters occupy the two half-sides, giving eight congruent semicircles.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 2,\n          \"fact\": \"The requested circle lies in the central gap, outside the semicircular disks, and touches their inward arcs. Thus the relevant tangencies are external, not internal.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q003\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_p148\", \"cnm_p089\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"special-right-triangles\",\n          \"label\": \"30-60-90 triangles\",\n          \"nodeIds\": [\"cnm_140\", \"cnm_174\"]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"special-angle-tangents\",\n          \"label\": \"Special-angle tangent ratios\",\n          \"nodeIds\": [\"cnm_276\", \"cnm_277\"]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"A: √3/6\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Let AD = CB = h > 0, so AB = DC = 2h. The trisectors give angle ADE = 30° and angle ADF = 60°. Either the 30° right-triangle property with Pythagoras or the special-angle tangent ratios gives AE = h/√3 and AF = √3h. Hence EF = 2h/√3. Triangle DEF has base EF and height h, so its area is h²/√3; the rectangle has area 2h². Their ratio is 1/(2√3) = √3/6. Both area scopes apply, and the two listed routes are valid alternatives rather than joint requirements.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 3,\n          \"fact\": \"The top side has points in the order A, E, F, B, while D and C are the lower-left and lower-right vertices. Thus EF = AF − AE.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 3,\n          \"fact\": \"Segments DE and DF join D to E and F on AB. Together with the stated rectangle and trisection conditions, this identifies the 30° and 60° right-triangle configurations and makes AD the height of triangle DEF above base EF.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}",
